# Development Workflow

## Git Practices
- Create feature branches from `main`
- Use descriptive branch names (e.g., `feature/score-calculation`)
- Write clear, descriptive commit messages
- Keep commits focused and atomic
- Rebase before creating pull requests
- Never force push to main branch

## Branch Naming Convention
- `feature/` - New features or enhancements
- `bugfix/` - Bug fixes
- `hotfix/` - Urgent production fixes
- `release/` - Release preparation
- `docs/` - Documentation updates

## Pull Request Process
1. Create a new branch for your feature
2. Make your changes
3. Run tests and ensure they pass
4. Update documentation if needed
5. Create a pull request
6. Request review from at least one team member
7. Address any feedback
8. Merge only after approval

## Code Review Guidelines
- Review code for functionality and quality
- Check for adherence to project standards
- Verify test coverage
- Ensure documentation is updated
- Look for potential security issues
- Check for performance implications

## Continuous Integration
- All tests must pass before merging
- Code coverage must meet minimum requirements
- Static analysis must pass
- Build must succeed on all platforms
- Documentation must be up-to-date

## Release Process
1. Create release branch
2. Update version numbers
3. Update changelog
4. Run final tests
5. Get approval from team
6. Merge to main
7. Tag release
8. Deploy to production
